'''''Pelargonium cucullatum''''' is a hairy, upright, branching, [[perennial]] shrub, of {{cvt|1-2|m|ft}} high, that has been assigned to the [[Geraniaceae|cranesbill family]]. It sprouts new stems from the underground rootstock and becomes woody at its base. It has [[Leaf#Arrangement on the stem|alternately set]], sometimes slightly [[succulent]] leaves crowded near the top of the branches, with leaf stalks and flat to hood-shaped leaf blades, with a rounded broad triangular to kidney-shaped outline of about {{cvt|4-5.5|cm|in|frac=5}} long and {{cvt|5-9|cm|in|frac=5}} wide, often somewhat incised, the margin with irregular teeth. The white to purplish red, [[Merosity|5-merous]], somewhat [[Zygomorph|mirror symmetrical]] flowers grow in umbel-like clusters, and each contain mostly 7 fertile stamens and 3 infertile staminodes (best checked in the bud) of different length. ''P. cucullatum'' has been cultivated as a garden ornamental and house plant since the 17th century. It has been used to [[plant breeding|breed]] many modern pelargonium hybrids, notably the [[Pelargonium#C. Regal pelargoniums (Pelargonium × domesticum Bailey)|Regal pelargoniums]]. It is called '''hooded-leaf pelargonium''' or '''herba althaea''' in English and '''wildemalva''' in [[Afrikaans]].<ref name=Volschenk /><ref name=PZA>{{cite web|website= SANBI PlantZAfrica|title= Pelargonium cucullatum|author= Liesl May|url= http://pza.sanbi.org/pelargonium-cucullatum}}</ref>

== Description ==
''Pelargonium cucullatum'' is a hairy, upright, branching, perennial shrub of {{cvt|1-2|m|ft}} high, with a taproots and underground runners from which intermittent shoots arise. It is fragrant when rubbed. The branches are initially herbaceous and greyish green but become eventually woody and brown. These are {{cvt|6-10|mm|in}} in diameter, and have a sparse to dense covering of long, soft ([[Trichome#Aerial surface hairs|villous]]) hairs or straight hairs all pointing in the same direction (also called [[Trichome#Aerial surface hairs|strigose]]), and  some [[Trichome#Glandular trichomes|glandular hairs]]. The [[Leaf#Arrangement on the stem|alternate]] leaves are crowding near the ends of branches and are hairy in the same manner as the branches. Each leaf is accompanied by two free, [[Dehiscence (botany)|caducous]], membranous, light green, ovate to narrowly ovate [[stipule]]s of {{cvt|5-10|mm|in}} long and {{cvt|3-7|mm|in}} wide with a pointy tip, to the sides of the [[Petiole (botany)|leaf stem]]. The leaf stem is mostly {{cvt|20-55|mm|in}} long (full range {{cvt|8-90|mm|in}}), with a groove on the somewhat flattened upper side. The undivided leaf blade is flat to cup-shaped, with a firm to somewhat succulent, rounded, broadly triangular to kidney-shaped outline of about {{cvt|4-5.5|cm|in|frac=5}} long and {{cvt|5-9|cm|in|frac=5}} wide, often somewhat incised, the margin with irregular teeth, particularly near the base, occasionally accentuated by a red line or a row of hairs, and a heart-shaped to wedge-shaped base. The [[Leaf#Veins|veins]] are sunken below the upper leaf surface but stick out on the lower leaf surface.<ref name=Volschenk />

The [[inflorescence]] is a branched flowering stem that bears up to 4 [[umbel]]s with mostly 3-9, but sometimes as few as only one or as much as 13 flowers each. The flowering stem bears one or two small leaves and two to four green bracts at each branching. These bracts are oval to broadly oval with a pointy tip, {{cvt|5-9|mm|in}} long and {{cvt|2-4|mm|in}} wide, ovate  to  broadly  ovate  with  acute  apices, sparsely pilose to villous (especially abaxially and at the margins). The stems carrying the umbels (called [[Peduncle (botany)|peduncle]]s) are green or tinged red, with many soft hairs and fewer glandular hairs, mostly {{cvt|3-6|cm|in}} long (full range {{cvt|2-7|cm|in}}) mm long. The peduncles are sometimes slightly curved when the flowers are stil the in bud, become upright when the flowers are open, and recurve or nod after flowering. The stems of the individual flowers (or [[Pedicel (botany)|pedicel]]s) are green or reddish brown, felty hairy and {{cvt|2-7|mm|in}} or rarely up to {{cvt|11|mm|in}} mm long.<ref name=Volschenk />

As in all ''Pelargonium'' species, the posterior sepal is fused with the pedicel forming the nectary tube or hypanthium, and in ''P. cucullatum'', it is {{cvt|5-12|mm|in}} long, felty hairy, green or reddish brown. The five [[sepal]]s are felty hairy, green or reddish brown in colour, {{cvt|12-20|mm|in}} long and {{cvt|2-6|mm|in}} wide, narrowly elliptic to elliptic with pointy tips. The five petals are dark pinkish, light pink or rarely white in colour, {{cvt|15-32|mm|in}} long and {{cvt|6-17|mm|in}} wide. Two larger [[petal]]s on the upper side of the flower are asymmetric [[Glossary of leaf morphology#obovate|inverted egg-shaped]], with dark purple streaks and a reddish purple tinge at the base that dissolves in reddish purple patches. The three lower and smaller petals are narrowly elliptic to inverted egg-shaped, {{cvt|15-28|mm|in}} long and {{cvt|6-12|mm|in}} wide, and marked reddish purple. The 10 filaments are white to pale pink in colour and merged at base. The filaments differ in length. Mostly 7 (rarely fewer) of them carry {{cvt|2-3|mm|in}} long, purple [[anther]]s, fixed at the centre that open with a slit towards the centre of the flower, exposing orange [[pollen]]. Two are {{cvt|12-18|mm|in}}, two {{cvt|10-16|mm|in}}, two {{cvt|8-13|mm|in}} and one {{cvt|11-15|mm|in}} long. Three (rarely more) are staminodes that lack anthers and are {{cvt|5-10|mm|in}} long. The reddish purple style is {{cvt|6-9|mm|in}} long, with few long, straight, soft, hairs in the lower half and five dark reddish purple [[Stigma (botany)|stigmas]] of {{cvt|2-4|mm|in}} long. The fruits each consist of five [[mericarp]]s of {{cvt|15-31|mm|in}} long, with a capsule at base of {{cvt|4-6|mm|in}} long and a tail of {{cvt|11-25|mm|in}} long. The capsules contain one seed each of {{cvt|3-4|mm|in}} long.<ref name=Volschenk />
